## Decisions

**Seed-semantic vs canonical-template is the load-bearing distinction.** The spec's `## Strategy` section codifies two idempotency modes and names the rule for picking between them: *who owns the bytes after first write.* Playbook hub and vault CLAUDE.md are user-owned the moment they land — the user adds routing rows, wikilinks, navigation notes. The MCP prescription is tool-owned forever — a config value the tool prescribes, so byte identity is the correct signal. This asymmetry is intentional and permanent; the architect codifies it in the orchestrator JSDoc (step 3) so future readers see why two different idempotency implementations coexist in one module.

**No shared comparator helper between the two modes.** A tempting refactor would be to introduce a `compareOrExists(filePath, template, mode)` helper that switches on `mode: "seed" | "canonical"`. The spec forbids this explicitly: "No shared comparator between the two modes. The architect must not route both through a single `compareOrExists`-style helper — that hides the semantic distinction." The distinct failure modes (a byte-compare on seed content is a data-loss bug; a presence-only check on MCP config is a stale-config bug) justify distinct code paths. The two seed-file helpers call `exists()` directly; `buildObsidianMcpStep` keeps its bespoke JSON-parse-and-args-tail comparison.

**`compareBytes` stays in the shared folder, just unused by this module.** Removing `compareBytes` from `@/tools/init/shared/` would be out of scope and would break other callers (methodology docs, versions.json, per-file canonical comparisons elsewhere in the init pipeline). The plan removes only the import from `provisionBrain/index.ts` — the helper itself is untouched.

**No new InitStep status variant.** The spec explicitly considered this and rejected it: "Do not introduce a new status variant unless there's a strong architectural reason." The existing `"exists"` and `"would-create"` are sufficient — seed presence maps to `"exists"`, seed absence maps to `"would-create"`. Introducing a `"seed-exists"` variant would force downstream code (`applySteps`, the init command prompt, integration tests) to branch on a distinction that matters only inside `provisionBrain`. The InitStep consumers only need to know *apply* vs *skip*; the idempotency reason is the helper's business.

**Drift tests are rewritten, not deleted.** The two existing drift tests are valuable test coverage — they already set up the drifted-file state correctly. Rewriting their assertions (rather than deleting them and adding net-new tests) preserves the investment in the setup code and gives the implementor a concrete before/after to verify against. The new negative-assertion regression test in step 6 complements them with parameterized exhaustive coverage.

**`init.md` is out of scope.** The init command's generic overwrite-branch prompt (lines 80-95) fires on any `would-overwrite` step in a category. After this plan, the brain seed files never produce that status, so the prompt is naturally no longer triggered for them. The MCP sub-step can still produce `would-overwrite` (e.g., when the obsidian entry has a stale path) — and in that case the generic prompt is exactly what should fire. Step 8 is a verify-only step that confirms this by reading `init.md` and produces no edit; a separate plan is spun up only if verification surprises the implementor.

**Playbook note references in Read lists.** The plan references three playbook notes across its steps: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` (function ordering, default-export-last, early-return style — all the code-hygiene conventions the implementor will apply while editing), `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow` (function signatures, single input objects, self-contained functions — governs how the two helpers look after the flip), and `coding-playbook/workflow/testing` (Vitest patterns, co-located tests, parameterized test style — governs the new and rewritten test cases). These three cover every edit in the plan without forcing the implementor to load the entire playbook.
